 \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 The comparison was only intended as a funny observation, but I found it so intriguing that I decided to go deeper down that rabbit hole. \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 After chewing and devouring tons of novels and movies, in this book I perform a similar exercise\u8212?but instead of doing it in one page, I do it in 195. I also include mythical associations, practical advice for writers, and examples from famous works of fiction. \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 {\b
Why 195 stages? Why not 196?} \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 If the Hero\u8217?s Journey is described in too few steps\u8212?like in the circular graphic at the beginning of this introduction\u8212?it fails to reflect the parallels between different stories in a useful level of detail. For a critic or a writer, twelve, fifteen, or seventeen stages fall way too short. \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 On the other extreme, if you atomize the steps too much (I have seen versions with literally{\i
thousand}{\i
s} of stages), it all becomes repetitive and blurry. You end up with too many stages that apply to some stories but not to others, which means that many of those stages are not truly fundamental. \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 {\i
So, why 195? } \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 Because that\u8217?s the number I came to after distilling the stages that are truly essential, the ones that describe the mythical Hero\u8217?s Journey as presented in modern bestsellers and blockbusters. \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 {\b
A World in four acts} \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 English writer E. M. Forster (1879-1970) said, \u8220?\u8216?The king died and then the queen died\u8217? is a story. \u8216?The king died, and then the queen died of grief\u8217? is a plot.\u8221? \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 I respectfully disagree. \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 \u8220?The king died and then the queen died\u8221? is a chronology. \u8220?The king died, and then the queen died of grief\u8221? is almost a plot, but not quite. \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 So what\u8217?s missing? \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 Well, in their most elemental form, stories (or plots) have three parts: \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 1) Something happens (Act I \u8211? Setup). \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 2) Someone does something about it (Act II \u8211? Confrontation). \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 3) Either things change for good, or not (Act III \u8211? Resolution). \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 Anything less than this, and what you have is just a chronicle. For that reason, in this book I begin by dividing the Hero\u8217?s Journey as follows: \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 Act \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 Stages \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 What happens here: \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
