 {\b
Are all the 195 stages always there?} \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 I am confident in saying that at least eighty percent of them are always there. The sequence changes, the characters change, the symbolism changes, but the stages are there. \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 What\u8217?s more, stories that leave too many stages out will probably feel hollow and incomplete, because they will not fully resonate with the mythical structure that we have engrained in our minds. \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 If you are a writer (or aspiring writer), I encourage you to include as many of these stages as possible in your story, even if some stages are represented by just one sentence. The exception would be those stages that only present backstory.{\i
The Matrix}{\i
,}for example, doesn\u8217?t deal with Neo\u8217?s infancy; it simply starts when the hero is already an adult (like most stories do). \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 {\b
Stages versus scenes} \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 How much space, in paragraphs or pages, should each stage take in your novel? \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 If you write one page for each stage in this book, you have a 195-page novel right there. But it doesn\u8217?t work like that. \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 Some stages might take several pages, and others might only need a single sentence. It all depends on the relative importance of each stage in a particular story. \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 Consider the following, though: \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 {\b
The general sequence}{\b
.} The narrative timeline may jump around, which sometimes complicates things for both the writer and the reader, but the story has to have a consistent causal chain. The Call to Adventure can only happen after the Hero meets the Mentor, the Final Battle must take place (or resolve) at the end, and so on. \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 {\b
Some scenes represent more than one stage}{\b
.}Movies and novels have sixty scenes on average. So each scene comprises about three stages. For example, when Han Solo joins the battle at the end of{\i
Star War}{\i
s}:{\i
Episode IV - A New Hope}{\i
,} three different stages are represented by that one action (stages 165, 166, and 167 of this book). \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 {\b
Some stages don\u8217?t translate directly into scenes}{\b
.} These stages act as arcs, moods, motifs, or themes. This is where the authors use their talent to avoid rigidity and transcend clich\u233?s. \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 {\b
Clich\u233?s are the enemy} \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 The 195 stages form the master structure of great stories. But two pitfalls lie ahead: \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 {\b
Predictability}{\b
.} When I watch a movie, I always know when the Mentor is going to die, whether or not the Hero is going to pass a particular test, and so on. In fact, I am gladly surprised when something happens that I didn\u8217?t see coming. And every good story should have an element of surprise. \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 {\b
Unconscious plagiarism}{\b
.}If you write a novel following the stages to the letter, you may end up with a rip-off of{\i
Harry Potte}{\i
r}or{\i
Star War}{\i
s} on your hands\u8212?and a lawsuit at your doorstep. That would be a pity, because even if all stories are fundamentally similar, there is a myriad of great ways to tell them, waiting to be discovered by the next imaginative author. \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 Take advantage of the 195 stages, but stay creative because novels written by numbers only mimic the real thing. Don\u8217?t just follow the stages; make them sing and dance for you. \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 {\b
How to use The Ultimate Hero\u8217?s Journey} \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 {\b
Use it to plot}{\b
.} You can use the stages either to develop the big picture of your story or to define scenes to the finest detail\u8212?or both (which is what I do). \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 {\b
Use it to defeat writer\u8217?s block}{\b
.} Getting lost in Act Two is pretty common because it is usually the longest act. But with this book you will never get stuck there again; just jump to the next stage and continue writing or plotting from there. Later on, everything will fall into place as you fill any remaining holes. \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 {\b
Use it to become a better critic}{\b
.} You don\u8217?t have to write a novel or a script, of course; you can simply read this book for learning and enjoyment. But something interesting will happen: You will become a sharper observer. Granted, you will be harder to please, because weak passages and plot holes will be more evident to you. But you will also gain appreciation for subtle, masterful touches. I promise, after reading this book, you\u8217?ll enjoy everything you read or watch at a whole new level. \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 {\b
Types of stages} \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 {\b
{\ul
Plot points}} \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 These stages mark a change of direction in the story and, consequently, the beginning of a new act: \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
