 119 \u8211? Second Epiphany \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 142 \u8211? Third Epiphany \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 Note that all these represent epiphanies. Epiphanies emerge from revelations: It\u8217?s the acquisition of new knowledge or information that generates the change. These stages are printed on{\b
gra}{\b
y} pages. \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 {\b
{\ul
Repetitive stages}} \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 A great story takes the Hero\u8212?and the audience\u8212?to new, exciting landscapes. Every time a border is crossed (like a police checkpoint, a frontier, a passage between dimensions, or just a door), the Hero does the following: \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 {\b
Encounter}{\b
s} {\b
a} {\b
guardian}{\b
.} It can be a security check, a centurion, a monster, a padlock, a password, or just a lazy dog sleeping in the way, belly up. These guardians are tests. \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 {\b
Discover}{\b
s} {\b
ne}{\b
w} {\b
things}{\b
.} New smells, different colors, changing seasons, strange animals, or just underwear on the floor; in short, the Hero encounters things relevant to that new place. \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 {\b
Evolve}{\b
s}. And the Hero\u8217?s external appearance reflects this inner growth. The Hero might change into new clothes or a disguise, armor, a space suit, a swimsuit, etc. \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 Now and then, we all fantasize about disappearing for a while, and transitions are a great chance to allow your readers to go on an imaginary trip. Use that opportunity well, and they will love you for it. \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 Consider reintroducing these stages whenever the setting changes: \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
