{\qc
 194 \u8211? The Open End \par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
{\qc
 {\b
{\i
Some element of the dark side remains. }} \par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 {\b
Description:} \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 If your story will have an open end (i.e., evil might return), the challenge lies in making it subtle. \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 This is easier to pull off in a movie than in a novel, because, how do you write an open end without diluting the satisfaction that the novel\u8217?s end must provide? That is what we writers have to figure out. \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 {\b
Examples:} \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 {\b
{\i
Sta}}{\b
{\i
r}} {\b
{\i
War}}{\b
{\i
s}}{\b
:} Darth Vader is shown regaining control of his fighter. He will be back for another seven films (and counting). \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 {\b
{\i
Th}}{\b
{\i
e}} {\b
{\i
Matri}}{\b
{\i
x}}{\b
:} Even when there is a new sheriff in town, the Matrix persists. The fight continues. \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 {\b
{\i
Harr}}{\b
{\i
y}} {\b
{\i
Potte}}{\b
{\i
r}}{\b
:}At the end of{\i
The Sorcerer\u8217?s Ston}{\i
e}, things are just starting. Voldemort survived, of course, to battle it out in seven more movies. \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 {\b
{\i
Sideway}}{\b
{\i
s}}{\b
:} The end of this movie mirrors the uncertain ending of Miles\u8217?s novel. We see him knocking at Maya\u8217?s door, but we don\u8217?t see anything else. What happens after that is a mystery. Talk about subtlety. \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
 {\b
{\i
Dodgebal}}{\b
{\i
l}}{\b
:} In the post-credits sequence, White Goodman, obese and abandoned to excess, sings a rap while holding a piece of fried chicken in his hand. (Is he going to come back? Nah.) \par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
